      "text": "To determine the value of \\( a \\) such that the given lines are perpendicular, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the slope of each line.**\n\n   The first equation is given in slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) is the slope. For the first line:\n   \\[\n   y = 2x + 13\n   \\]\n   the slope \\( m_1 \\) is \\( 2 \\).\n\n   The second equation is given in standard form \\( Ax + By = C \\). To find the slope, we need to rewrite it in slope-intercept form. Start by isolating \\( y \\):\n   \\[\n   6y + ax = 6\n   \\]\n   Subtract \\( ax \\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   6y = -ax + 6\n   \\]\n   Divide every term by 6:\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n   \\]\n   The slope \\( m_2 \\) of the second line is \\( -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\n2. **Determine the condition for perpendicularity.**\n\n   Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\( -1 \\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n   \\]\n   Substitute the slopes \\( m_1 = 2 \\) and \\( m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the fraction:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n   \\]\n   Multiply both sides by \\(-3\\) to solve for \\( a \\):\n   \\[\n   a = 3\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
