      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) that makes the given lines perpendicular, we need to find the slopes of both lines and ensure that the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\).\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line given by the equation \\(y = 2x + 13\\).\n\nThe equation \\(y = 2x + 13\\) is already in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope. Therefore, the slope \\(m_1\\) of this line is:\n\\[\nm_1 = 2\n\\]\n\nNext, let's convert the second equation \\(6y + ax = 6\\) into slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\).\n\nStarting with the equation \\(6y + ax = 6\\), we isolate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n6y = -ax + 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n\\]\n\nFrom this equation, we can see that the slope \\(m_2\\) of this line is:\n\\[\nm_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\n\\]\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\\[\nm_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n\\]\nSubstituting the slopes we found:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side:\n\\[\n-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(-3\\) to solve for \\(a\\):\n\\[\na = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
